  "rules": [
    "Model forecasts exhibit a severe underestimation bias during high-volatility regimes, consistently predicting gradual linear growth while true outcomes show sharp upward spikes followed by mean reversion.",
    "When sentiment history contains isolated positive signals (e.g., 0.25 or 0.50) amidst otherwise neutral data, the model fails to incorporate this as a catalyst for immediate price acceleration.",
    "The model suffers from 'lagged reaction' errors: it only begins to align with true outcomes after the initial spike has occurred and momentum has stabilized, rather than anticipating the volatility event.",
    "In periods of high historical variance (standard deviation > 5), the model's fixed-slope projection leads to significant overshoot or undershoot depending on whether the true trend is a sudden breakout or a mean-reverting correction.",
    "The forecast horizon assumes smooth continuity; corrections are required to apply a volatility multiplier when recent history shows alternating peaks and troughs within a short window."
  ]
}
